== C. RESEARCH: Understand the Rhetorical Situation
=== \4. Who is the intended audience, and what is the artifact trying to help them accomplish?
The intended audience is minecraft players of all ages aiming to get more villagers.
=== \5. What does the artifact assume its audience already knows or understands?
The artifact assumes that the audience already knows the basic mechanics of the game, such as placing blocks, gathering resources, etc.

== D. ANALYZE: Return to the Design
=== Choose ONE specific design choice to examine more closely.
=== \6. What is the design choice, and how does it shape what users notice, understand, or do?
The use of chapters makes the video more scannable and easier to mentally map.
=== \7. How might this choice create difficulties for certain users?
It might cause difficulty for people who don't know about the chapters feature, and people on mobile who have a harder time navigating the chapters feature.

== E. IDENTIFY THE PROBLEM & REDESIGN
=== \8. Where is the mismatch between what the artifact needs to accomplish and what its current design actually does?
The artifact is supposed to be easily used while playing minecraft, but its current design as a landscape-mode video makes it hard to show on the same screen and harms performance on low-end devices.
=== \9. What specific change would you make to address this problem, and why?
I want to make the artifact more text-based and more resizable so it better fits on the screen and has better performance.
== F. WRITE: Build Your Analytical Paragraph
=== \10. Write one coherent analytical paragraph that connects:
=== Rhetorical situation: Audience and purpose
=== Design choice: What you observed
=== Analysis: How that choice affects users
=== Problem: The mismatch you identified
=== Redesign: Your proposed change and its rationale
=== Your paragraph should explain why a particular design choice matters, not simply describe what the artifact looks like.
The purpose of this video is to explain how to build a Villager Breeder in minecraft to an audience of intermediate-to-advanced minecraft players. One design choice was to make this video in fixed landscape mode. This choice forces users with only one monitor to either make the video very small to see their game screen or switch between the game and the video repeatedly. This causes a problem of making it harder to follow the instructions because they are hard to view concurrently with the game. My redesign would make the tutorial dynamically resizable, allowing it to fit in a tall, vertical format. This would allow users with a single monitor to split their screens with the tutorial taking only a small edge of the screen, maximizing playability while allowing the tutorial to be viewed.
